;
;       {_______            {_          {______
;             {__          {_ __               {__
;             {__         {_  {__               {__
;          {__           {__   {__               {__
;      {______          {__     {__              {__
;            {__       {__       {__            {__
;              {_________         {______________		Expansion Unit
;
;      gpu64 -- the same room, as a retained scene.
;
;      WHAT THIS PROGRAM IS FOR
;
;      gpu64_demo_quake.a draws this room with class 2. It holds the level
;      on the gpu64 side, but a FRAME is still eight commands and about
;      sixty register writes -- a camera, two lights, a face range, a
;      monster batch, a weapon sprite, a status bar, a flip -- and the C64
;      is halted for every one of them.
;
;      This program draws the same room with class 1, as a SCENE. The level
;      is a mesh node, the monsters are sprite nodes, the torch and the
;      muzzle flash are light nodes, and the camera is a node too. Nothing
;      here issues a draw call at all: LOOP_START hands the framebuffer to
;      gpu64, which renders the scene on core 1 at its own frame clock, and
;      a frame on the C64 side is at most
;
;              SET_POSITION + SET_ORIENTATION  on the camera,
;              SET_ORIENTATION                 on the monster that turns,
;              SET_POSITION + SET_ORIENTATION  on the monster that walks,
;              SCENE_COMMIT,
;
;      six commands of which five are a handful of register writes each.
;      "At most", because the camera is sent only when the player actually
;      moved: the room -- thirty triangles, ten textures, a directional
;      light, a point light, four nodes' worth of transform -- is resident
;      and is never sent again after start-up, and so is the camera. A
;      frame on which he stands still is four commands, three of them the
;      two monsters that are always moving.
;
;      Twice a second that is not quite true: one frame in thirty-two
;      re-sends the camera and one in thirty-two re-sends the level node,
;      unchanged or not. See sendCamera -- a dropped ARG or ID write is
;      invisible to SEQACK, and without this the scene would keep a wrong
;      camera pitch, or a displaced room, for the rest of the run.
;
;      WHAT IS DELIBERATELY MISSING
;
;      The weapon, and the status bar. Both were drawn over the finished 3D
;      view with class 0, and in handshake mode the render loop owns the
;      framebuffer: there is no moment between the loop's frames at which
;      the C64 may draw into the page that is about to be shown. That is a
;      real limitation of the stage 16 loop rather than an oversight, and
;      it is written up in docs/class1-3d-mesh-reference.md. The report goes
;      on the C64's own screen instead, which is where a bench reader can
;      photograph it anyway.
;
;      THE CONTROLS
;
;              W / S           walk forward and back
;              A / D           turn left and right
;              Q / E           strafe left and right
;              F1 / F3         look up and down, F7 centres
;              SPACE           fire -- a muzzle flash that lights the room
;              RUN/STOP        quit
;
;      WHAT THE C64 SCREEN SHOWS
;
;        SETUP    OK, or the number of the start-up command that failed and
;                 its ERRCODE. The steps are numbered in the order they are
;                 issued, from 1.
;        FRAMES   frames attempted.
;        OK       SCENE_COMMITs that answered OK. Commits are paced on
;                 STATUS bit 4 (FRAME_READY), so this should track FRAMES
;                 closely; committing flat out instead is ~80% BUSY by
;                 design, which is what the stage 16 loop test measured.
;        MISSED   dispatches whose SEQACK did not match the SEQ written for
;                 them AND still did not match on an immediate second read,
;                 so the firmware's state really is stale. Every node update
;                 here is re-sent from the top when this trips, so this
;                 counts detections and not lost frames. Then a verdict on
;                 the count, so a poor photograph is still readable:
;                 LO under 256 (run 30 reproduced), MID under 1024,
;                 HI at or above it (runs 28-29, ~3000).
;        ERR      ERRCODE of the last SCENE_COMMIT that was not OK, then
;                 ERRCODE reads that came back OUT OF RANGE and were
;                 therefore read a second time, then how many of those were
;                 still out of range, then the value that last second read
;                 gave. The valid codes run $00-$0C, so anything above that
;                 is the bus and not an answer: field 2 counts unreadable
;                 ANSWERS, not refused commands, and only field 3 is an
;                 answer actually lost. Run 15 reported 106 phantom
;                 failures for want of this split, and run 16 showed the
;                 bad value is not always $FF -- it was $20, with $D1 and
;                 $A3 elsewhere on the screen.
;        CLOCK    the measured frame period in microseconds, then how
;                 many of dmInit's eight GET_INFO attempts were unspent
;                 when the info block landed. 08 is a first-time hit; 00
;                 means the block never arrived and the period is not a
;                 measurement of anything.
;
;        STALLS   frames on which the render loop did not report a finished
;                 frame before the wait ran out. Should stay 00. Then the
;                 GET_HEALTH readback's own record, six fields:
;                   ff     attempts that failed verification, saturating
;                   ee     ERRCODE of the last of them
;                   mm     ... of which the magic was absent, so the block
;                          stopped arriving before its last four bytes
;                   ss     ... of which the magic was present and the
;                          XOR/sum disagreed, so the body is corrupt
;                   oo     first offset still holding the $5a poison on the
;                          most recent failure; 80 means only the trailer
;                          this build added failed to land, a low moving
;                          value means the body is short, FF means nothing
;                          was poisoned and the block is corrupt instead,
;                          and FE means there has been no failure at all
;                   vv     voidWin, A/B windows given up on entirely
;                 mm + ss must equal ff. 00 00 00 00 FF 00 is clean, and
;                 anything else is what to read rows 16-17 against -- a
;                 voided window means those rows are a partial run.
;
;      ...and the four rows at the bottom, filled in when the run ends,
;      which are what turn MISSED from a number into a diagnosis. They are
;      all DIFFERENCES across the run: GET_HEALTH is sampled once before the
;      first frame and once after the last.
;
;        BUS BAD  bad READS, bad WRITES, then the read-side address
;                 fingerprint (last, OR, AND) and the last bad write
;                 address, then the write-side OR and AND. A gpu64 register
;                 lives at $DF50-$DF68, so a correct sample has A4-A7 clear;
;                 a bad address names the bits that moved. Runs 16 and 17
;                 both gave AND = $80, i.e. A7 set in EVERY bad read
;                 address -- A7 being GPIO 3 in the g3 phase, the pin the
;                 LVC257 multiplexes with BUTTON, which idles high. The two
;                 write-side bytes were added to ask whether bad writes
;                 carry the same fingerprint.
;        BUS RW   gpu64-window reads, then writes, that the polling loop
;                 serviced, then the reads of an ARG register. These are the
;                 denominators MISSED never had. The third column is not a
;                 fault: `sta ARG,y` makes the 6502 emit a dummy read at the
;                 address it is about to write, and ARG registers are
;                 write-only, so every indexed argument store shows up
;                 there. The run of 2026-09-09 scored 50492 of these as bad
;                 reads before the counter was split out.
;        BUS BAD  of those, the ones at an address no gpu64 register
;                 occupies, reads then writes, then the last bad read
;                 address, the OR of every bad read address, the AND of
;                 every one, and the last bad write address. Non-zero means
;                 an access WAS serviced but at a mis-sampled address; A4-A7
;                 are multiplexed on the cartridge port so only the high
;                 nibble can move. An AND of FF with a count of 0000 is the
;                 no-samples sentinel.
;        DISPWHY  commands the firmware dispatched, then the SIGNED
;                 difference against what this program sent, then WHY a
;                 stuck run is stuck. Zero is a clean run; -NNNN counts
;                 commands whose CMD_LO write was never sampled, +NNNN
;                 counts commands the firmware ran that were never sent.
;                 The closing GET_HEALTH is already allowed for.
;
;                 This column printed the C64's raw count until 2026-09-10,
;                 when run 10's photograph could not be read: 021E67 against
;                 021E0F or 021EDF is +87 or -121, and those are opposite
;                 findings. Subtracting on the C64 puts a sign glyph on the
;                 screen instead, which a photograph cannot lose.
;
;                 The last four columns were added on 2026-09-10 after two
;                 bench runs that each entered a state they never left: CMT
;                 06 -- SCENE_COMMIT answering UNSUPPORTED -- on 2011 of
;                 2182 frames and then on 11832 of 17326, with the C64 side
;                 still running, still dispatching, and STALLS 00. That is
;                 a frozen HDMI picture behind a program that looks alive.
;                 $06 has four unrelated producers and the screen could not
;                 say which, so now it does:
;
;                   NOLOOP  SCENE_COMMITs refused because the class 1 loop
;                           was not running.
;                   NOMODE  class 1/2 commands refused because the display
;                           was not in graphics mode -- which refuses the
;                           node updates too, not just the commit.
;                   STATE   the flag byte at the LAST refusal, not at the
;                           end of the run (LOOP_STOP legitimately clears
;                           bit 0 before the closing GET_HEALTH). Bit 0
;                           loop running, 1 frame clock calibrated, 2
;                           graphics mode, 3 flip pending, 4 FRAME_READY,
;                           5 BUSY, 6 framebuffer up.
;                   WHY     what stopped the loop last: 01 the LOOP_STOP
;                           opcode, 02 SCENE_RESET, 03 a session teardown
;                           (resetREU, i.e. a C64 reset), 04 boot. At the
;                           end of a clean run this must read 01; anything
;                           else means the closing LOOP_STOP did not land.
;                   STOPTRY how many closing LOOP_STOPs SEQACK refused to
;                           confirm before one stuck. 00 is the normal
;                           reading.
;
;                 These rows normally appear only at finish. From
;                 2026-09-10 they also appear the moment 32 consecutive
;                 commits are refused, with STUCK - SNAPSHOT BELOW on row
;                 3 -- so a frozen run can be photographed as it stands,
;                 with no RUN/STOP and nothing lost. See stuckWatch.
;
;                 A run that ends in CMT 06 with NOLOOP large and WHY 01
;                 says a LOOP_STOP opcode executed that this program never
;                 issued mid-run -- which makes it a corrupted command, and
;                 puts it back on the known bus defect. NOMODE large says
;                 the same about a TEXT_MODE opcode. Both zero says the
;                 frame clock, and STATE bit 1 confirms it.
;        KEY      the tail of row 23, built 2026-09-10 out of bench run 14.
;                 Four bytes: refusals, THE OPCODE OF THE LAST ONE, cameras
;                 lost, scenes wiped. The second byte is what the run is
;                 for -- run 14 could prove the live scene's active camera
;                 had been destroyed but not say by which opcode, and the
;                 one-shot key in ARG15 now both refuses a phantom and
;                 records its name. See showKey.
;
;        STOPSRC  built 2026-09-10 out of bench run 11, which caught the
;                 freeze in the act and read NOLOOP 0020 WHY 01: a LOOP_STOP
;                 opcode had executed 30000 frames before this program's
;                 teardown. STOPSRC says where that opcode came from. It
;                 reads as: how far the sequence number moved for that
;                 dispatch, the opcode that ran immediately before it, and
;                 a verdict.
;
;                   00 ... PHANTOM  no command arrived at all -- the SEQ
;                                   write never happened, so CMD_LO was
;                                   written by something that was not this
;                                   program. Refusing a command whose SEQ
;                                   has not advanced would close the whole
;                                   class, and csDispatch already handles a
;                                   refusal by re-sending.
;                   01 ... REALCMD  a real command arrived and its DATA
;                                   byte was mis-sampled -- almost
;                                   certainly a SCENE_COMMIT's $08 latched
;                                   as $07. No SEQ gate can see that; it
;                                   would take a checksum or an echo.
;                   03 ... REALCMD  the same, on the one dispatch in 253
;                                   where seqCounter wraps $FE -> 01.
;                   xx ... REALCMD  with xx neither: commands were lost on
;                                   either side of it as well.
;                   .. .. IDLE      WHY is not 01, so these bytes are boot
;                                   leftovers and are not evidence.
;
;                 At finish this row describes the run's OWN closing
;                 LOOP_STOP and reads 01 08 REALCMD in a clean run -- the
;                 teardown is a real command, and the commit before it was
;                 the last thing sent. The evidentiary reading is the one
;                 stuckWatch freezes, and finish deliberately does not
;                 refresh any of these rows once it has.
;        SEQREP   dispatches that arrived with SEQ unchanged since the
;                 previous one -- i.e. the SEQ write was lost -- then the
;                 count of SEQACK reads that were wrong once and right
;                 immediately after, then how many of THOSE read $FF, then
;                 the value the second read gave, then GET_HEALTH's own
;                 ERRCODE. SEQREP reads 2 in a clean run, for the same two
;                 unsequenced dispatches.
;
;                 The $FF split is the one that matters: $FF is a floating
;                 bus, so those reads were never decoded to SEQACK -- a
;                 mis-sampled address. A wrong value that is NOT $FF came
;                 off a real register, and SEQBAD says which value.
;        SEQBAD   the non-$FF wrong reads, split three ways: how many held
;                 the PREVIOUS sequence number (the read beat its own
;                 dispatch -- a race), how many held the value the ERRCODE
;                 read two instructions earlier returned (the previous
;                 read's data still on the bus when this one latched), the
;                 last value that was neither, and the shared value at the
;                 last of those ERRCODE matches.
;
;                 The race bucket is dead: 1 event in 8322 frames. The
;                 ERRCODE bucket holds nearly all of the rest -- but read
;                 the fourth column before believing it. On 2026-09-10 it
;                 came back $00 in both runs, and $00 is what ERRCODE reads
;                 on every successful command, so "equalled the ERRCODE"
;                 and "read zero" are the same event and this split cannot
;                 tell them apart. What survives is the pair of rails: the
;                 bad SEQACK read answers $FF or $00 and almost nothing
;                 else.
;
;      So MISSED is attributed by reading DISP and SEQREP together:
;
;        DISPWHY short by MISSED    the CMD_LO writes were lost
;        DISPWHY clean, SEQREP up   the SEQ writes were lost
;        DISPWHY clean, SEQREP 1    nothing was lost on the write side
;
;      and the 2026-09-09 run said "some of each, and mostly neither": DISPWHY
;      short by 39, SEQREP up by 83, against MISSED 198 -- with MISSRD at
;      561, i.e. three times MISSED again in SEQACK reads that were wrong
;      once and right on an immediate re-read. The write side is real but is
;      not the biggest term; the read side is.
;
;      Run it: LOAD"GPU64-QUAKE3D",8,1 then RUN. RUN/STOP finishes.
;
;      Copyright (c) 2026 Honza Slesinger
;
